<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Hobson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Hobson was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> officer and the first governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Sent by the British government, he negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> with Māori chiefs on 6 February 1840 and proclaimed British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the islands. He chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> as capital and governed the new colony until his death in office in 1842.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-hobson.jpg" alt="William Hobson" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: McDonald, James Ingram, 1865-1935; Collins, James Edgell, 1820 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Hobson_portrait_by_James_Ingram_McDonald_1913.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
